# Barrier method

`BarrierMethod` minimizes a scalar objective subject to linear inequalities
`A x <= b`. It solves a sequence of unconstrained log-barrier problems with a
configurable inner solver. An automatic Phase I searches for a strictly feasible
point when the supplied start lies on or outside the boundary.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/barrier_method/struct.BarrierMethod.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                        |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
  | Objective   | A differentiable scalar function inside the feasible region        |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient`, plus a compatible `WarmStart` inner |
  | Search      | Local continuation through barrier subproblems                     |
  | Constraints | A linear inequality block with a nonempty strict interior          |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends with a compatible inner                    |

## When to use it

Use this wrapper when your constraints are linear inequalities and you want to
reuse an unconstrained solver. It follows the barrier and feasibility methods in
[Boyd and Vandenberghe (2004)](#ref-boyd-vandenberghe-2004), §§11.3–11.4.1.
Convex objectives give the usual central-path interpretation; on a nonconvex
objective, the inner solves remain local searches.

The complete declaration must contain only a linear inequality block. Bounds,
equalities, and nonlinear blocks are unsupported. For box bounds alone, compare
[L-BFGS-B](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs-b/) or [projected gradient
descent](../../first-order/projected-gradient-descent/). For mixed constraint
blocks, compare [SLSQP](../slsqp/). Linear equalities have a separate [augmented
Lagrangian method](../augmented-lagrangian-method/).

## How it works

Phase II minimizes `phi_mu(x) = f(x) - mu * sum(log(b[i] - a[i]^T x))`. Each
subproblem starts at the current point with a fresh inner run. Once that point
sufficiently centers the subproblem, the wrapper divides `mu` by the reduction
factor and repeats. The barrier returns positive infinity outside the strict
domain, encouraging accepted iterates to stay inside it.

If the start is not strictly feasible, Phase I first minimizes an auxiliary
feasibility objective. Basin eliminates the auxiliary scalar analytically, so
the inner solver still uses the original parameter type. As soon as a strict
point appears, Phase II starts with the initial barrier weight.

For an exactly centered convex subproblem, `m * mu` bounds objective
suboptimality, where `m` is the number of inequalities. Basin checks numerical
centering separately; a small scheduled gap alone does not establish that bound
for an approximate inner solution.

## Example

This quadratic has its unconstrained minimum at `[2.0, 2.0]`. The inequality
`x[0] + x[1] <= 2.0` moves the solution to `[1.0, 1.0]`, with cost `2.0`. The
example starts strictly inside the region and uses Armijo backtracking to keep
the inner search from accepting steps through the barrier.

<!-- compile: solver-barrier-method -->
```rust
use basin::{
    Backtracking, BarrierMethod, Constrained, ConstraintSet, CostFunction,
    DenseMatrix, Executor, Gradient, GradientDescent, LinearBlock,
    TerminationCode,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 2.0).powi(2) + (x[1] - 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Quadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![2.0 * (x[0] - 2.0), 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0)])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let constraints =
        ConstraintSet::new().with_linear_inequalities(LinearBlock::new(
            DenseMatrix::from_row_slice(1, 2, &[1.0, 1.0]),
            vec![2.0],
        ));
    let problem = Constrained::new(Quadratic, constraints);
    let inner = GradientDescent::with_line_search(Backtracking::new())
        .with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
    let solver = BarrierMethod::with_inner_solver(inner);
    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![0.0, 0.0])
        .max_iter(50)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert_eq!(result.report.code(), TerminationCode::SolverConverged);
    let x = result.param();
    assert!(x[0] + x[1] < 2.0);
    assert!((x[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-4);
    assert!((x[1] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-4);
    assert!((result.cost() - 2.0).abs() < 1e-4);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Choose an inner solver that respects the barrier

Pair gradient descent, BFGS, or unbounded L-BFGS with
[`Backtracking`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/backtracking/struct.Backtracking.html).
A fixed step can cross the boundary, and strong Wolfe searches can stall at the
infinity wall. Gradient-descent momentum can also carry an iterate outside the
region because its velocity lies outside the line search's control.

Inners implement `WarmStart` and optimize a `CostFunction + Gradient` surrogate.
Least-squares inners need residuals and a Jacobian and cannot consume this
adapter. A derivative-free inner can find feasible points, but Phase I needs a
gradient convergence certificate to report an empty strict interior. The
original problem still needs `Gradient` for independent centering checks.

### Allow enough work for centering

The defaults are `mu0 = 1.0`, reduction factor `10.0`, and `50` inner iterations
per segment. `with_absolute_duality_gap_tolerance` tests `m * mu` and defaults
to `1e-8`. Centering additionally requires a surrogate-gradient infinity norm at
most `1e-8`, or a reduction to `1e-3` of its initial norm at that weight.

Configure those guards with `with_absolute_stationarity_tolerance` and
`with_relative_stationarity_tolerance`. They combine with OR. `None` disables an
optional test; disabling both stationarity guards permits gap-only continuation
and stopping. Zero requests exact stationarity.

A capped inner solve supplies a candidate and retries the same weight until
centering passes. Near active constraints, the barrier can be ill conditioned.
Choose attainable tolerances, scale variables and constraint rows, and budget
outer iterations as well as inner segments. Independent verification adds up to
two counted gradient calls per consumed Phase II result. Executor budgets are
checked between outer steps, so a segment can exceed a remaining allowance.

### Require a strict interior

A feasible set confined to a boundary has no strict interior. If a centered
Phase I subproblem reaches `with_absolute_phase_one_gap_tolerance` (default
`1e-8`) without finding a strict point, the solver reports `SolverFailed`. That
decision has a numerical scale: finite precision cannot distinguish an empty
interior from an arbitrarily thin one. Exhausting an inner iteration budget
alone does not establish infeasibility.

## Implementation

`PointState` publishes the original objective at strictly feasible points and
positive infinity elsewhere. Phase I rejects points outside the strict domain
without calling the original objective or gradient. Only strictly feasible
points with eligible costs can establish an incumbent, so use checked state
access when a run stops before finding one. The outer state exposes no gradient.

Each inner solve starts fresh. Fresh outer initialization resets the phase,
weight schedule, and progress; an `ExactCheckpoint` retains them along with the
inner solver and evaluation counts. Enable `with_inner_diagnostics(true)` and
use `run_with_solver` to inspect the last attempted segment, including its
phase, weight, gap, and native outcome. With `serde`, solver checkpoints are
serializable when their constituent types are.

Supported dense pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`, when the chosen inner supports
them. The outer method uses matrix-vector products and transpose products
without a linear solve. Custom inners can add requirements; the default `Vec`
example needs no BLAS/LAPACK provider.

